# **Probability**
#
# Probability is central to evaluating user behaviour. Several KPIs are interpreted as empirical probabilities.
#
# For example, **Completion Rate**:
#
# $$P(\text{Completion}) = \frac{\text{Number of users reaching Confirm}}{\text{Total users}}$$
#
# This represents the probability that a randomly selected client successfully completes the digital process.
#
# Similarly, **Error Rate**:
#
# $$P(\text{Error}) = \frac{\text{Backward Navigation Events}}{\text{Total Navigation Events}}$$
#
# This estimates the probability that a randomly selected interaction contains user confusion or a navigation error.
#
# These probabilities provide intuitive measures of customer behaviour and support informed business decisions.
#
# **Inferential Statistics**
#
# Descriptive statistics alone cannot determine whether differences between the Test and Control groups occurred by chance. Therefore, inferential statistical methods are used to evaluate whether observed differences are statistically significant.
